From e18bf3d8f9d979f46828488a9652c9f70ee1fa60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 12:46:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] agentHost: fail ownerless Claude client tool calls
 instead of hanging

A client tool (mcp__client__<name>) runs on whichever connected window
contributed it. When no connected client provides the tool, the Claude
session stamped no contributor on ChatToolCallStart, so nothing published
a ToolClientExecution request, the disconnect grace timer never armed, and
the in-process MCP handler parked forever on the pending registry. The turn
hung. Copilot already fails such calls immediately; this mirrors it.

The mapper now records at content_block_start whether a client tool has an
owner and, at content_block_stop, follows ChatToolCallReady with a
ChatToolCallComplete carrying success: false and the toolUnavailable error
code the workbench uses for the same situation. Inner subagent tool uses
get the same completion from emitInnerAssistantSignals, tagged with their
parent. The session recognises that completion as it re-fires mapper
signals and answers the SDK up front: the permission ask is pre-answered,
because the host drops a ready for an already-failed call without replying,
and the parked MCP handler receives an isError result via respondOrBuffer,
so its later register resolves immediately. The SDK's own tool_result still
maps to a second completion, which the reducer and the tool-call tracker
already ignore. The permission pre-answer becomes redundant once
dispatchCanUseTool allows client tools early (#334037).

Tests cover the top-level and subagent paths in the mapper and the subagent
signals, and end to end through ClaudeAgent, where the parked handler and
the permission request are shown to settle.
