From 7b40b4f2a6828ee03de84c610ffc8646d34e9fbc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 13:39:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] agentHost: stop leaking ownerless client tool answers

Review follow-up on the ownerless client tool failure. The session
pre-answered the permission request and buffered the failure result the
moment the mapper failed the call, and both writes were unconditional. In
any permission mode that never calls canUseTool, the approval then sat in
the registry's early results until the session was disposed, and repeated
ownerless calls grew it for the session lifetime.

The permission answer is no longer buffered at all. The session records the
ownerless tool call ids, and requestPermission short-circuits them by
returning allow without parking or raising a confirmation, so nothing is
written to the permission registry whatever the mode does. The failure
result must still be buffered, because the in-process MCP handler resolves
through it, so it is swept when the turn completes; that covers modes which
deny before execution and never run the handler. PendingRequestRegistry
gains discardBufferedResult for that sweep.

Gating the pre-answer on the permission mode was considered and rejected.
The SDK documents acceptEdits as auto-accepting file edit operations only,
so a client tool still reaches canUseTool in that mode, and skipping the
pre-answer there would restore the hang this fix removed. Keying off the
tool call id is mode-independent and needs no such enumeration.

The two strings the failure carries are now localized with the tool name as
a placeholder, matching the surrounding tool-display code. The error code
stays a bare constant, since it is protocol, not UI text.

The two end-to-end tests now drive the permission ask and the MCP handler
mid-turn, where the SDK raises them, and a new test asserts that an
ownerless call whose handler never runs leaves behind neither a buffered
failure nor a buffered approval.
